---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
const pageUrl = (path) => `${base}${path}`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Anirudh Bompada"
  description="How Anirudh Bompada approaches data engineering and independent technical experiments."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Understand the system, <span class="accent">then improve it.</span></h1>
    <p class="page-intro">
      I like questions that lead to something testable: how a data workflow holds up,
      what a configuration changes, or which tradeoff makes a system easier to run.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="content-grid">
    <aside>
      <p>PROFILE / 001</p>
      <p>METHOD / BUILD + MEASURE</p>
      <p>GOAL / CLEARER SYSTEMS</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        I start by understanding the input, the intended result, and the constraints in
        between. Then I build a small, observable path to test the assumptions before
        treating the first working version as finished.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same approach carries across my professional data work and my independent
        local-AI experiments. They are different kinds of work, but both benefit from
        repeatable tests and notes that make a decision easier to revisit.
      </p>

      <h2>What guides the work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand the tradeoff, not just the recommended setting.</li>
        <li>Measure actual behavior before drawing conclusions.</li>
        <li>Document the path so the result can be reproduced later.</li>
        <li>Prefer clear systems that are easier to operate and improve.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <a class="text-link" href={pageUrl('career')}>Explore my professional work</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="text-link" href={pageUrl('projects/llm-benchmark')}>See an independent experiment</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
